CDS Performance AI:

CDS performance tuning in ABAP focuses on maximizing code pushdown to the database and leveraging the SAP HANA optimizer using specific design principles and analysis tools. 
Design Principle:
VDM Layering Structure views into Foundation, Building Block, and Consumption layers to promote reusability and modularity, avoiding overly deep view stacks (more than 10-15 layers).
Selective Retrieval:
Explicitly select only necessary fields and apply filters (using WHERE clauses and parameters) as early and low as possible in the view hierarchy to reduce the dataset size.
Optimize Joins and Associations:
Use inner joins where appropriate, define cardinality accurately to help the database optimizer, and include all shared fields in the ON clause for faster performance. Avoid unnecessary outer joins.
Perform Calculations Efficiently :
Execute expensive operations and aggregations after data reduction through filtering. Use CDS Table Functions (AMDP) for complex, procedural logic that must run on the database.
Utilize Annotations:
Employ annotations like @AbapCatalog.buffering for frequently accessed master data and @ObjectModel.usageType to provide hints to the optimizer about the view's primary use case (transactional, analytical, or search).
Avoid Anti-Patterns :
Minimize the use of complex CASE statements within views and resist the urge to recreate complex ABAP loops in CDS expressions.
Use Analysis Tools:
For performance analysis, use ABAP Development Tools (ADT) in Eclipse for the Dependency Analyzer and Data Preview. For deeper database-level analysis, use the SQL Trace (ST05 or ST12) and the HANA Plan Visualizer (PlanViz) to understand the query execution plan. 
